[{:db/id "c8fe49c8-710f-5b92-9a08-bb94ad638ee0" :answer/id #uuid "c8fe49c8-710f-5b92-9a08-bb94ad638ee0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 9 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d0eaa6cc-4368-55af-b243-68c7b79a2981" :answer/id #uuid "d0eaa6cc-4368-55af-b243-68c7b79a2981" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :answer/id #uuid "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "29e90636-c836-54ff-81ae-058a934ee6be" :answer/id #uuid "29e90636-c836-54ff-81ae-058a934ee6be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622" :answer/id #uuid "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["c8fe49c8-710f-5b92-9a08-bb94ad638ee0" "d0eaa6cc-4368-55af-b243-68c7b79a2981" "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" "29e90636-c836-54ff-81ae-058a934ee6be" "8a6cb252-46c3-558d-bcc6-d52eee217622"] :answer-field/correct "ea0102e5-7476-5f6a-84aa-b7f10ad8f376" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" :question/id #uuid "e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=1.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=1}\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 1)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[\\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(9 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=9 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["1e2f5e54-e210-5fdc-8326-8e2ab4e24cf5"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "a13070d2-5fd5-5d85-a19f-8d85e967eafc" :answer/id #uuid "a13070d2-5fd5-5d85-a19f-8d85e967eafc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" :answer/id #uuid "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :answer/id #uuid "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "52609f73-07a8-520a-a473-dcfa11de11b9" :answer/id #uuid "52609f73-07a8-520a-a473-dcfa11de11b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "064c81e9-cac4-5b54-aab3-071b92c05f88" :answer/id #uuid "064c81e9-cac4-5b54-aab3-071b92c05f88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 36 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "eb448220-651b-59de-aca5-26472ab8b179" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb448220-651b-59de-aca5-26472ab8b179" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["a13070d2-5fd5-5d85-a19f-8d85e967eafc" "776a83db-0de1-5556-a705-63fc901967dc" "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" "52609f73-07a8-520a-a473-dcfa11de11b9" "064c81e9-cac4-5b54-aab3-071b92c05f88"] :answer-field/correct "9ca28f88-96a9-529c-be5e-23330f3a20ea" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" :question/id #uuid "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["eb448220-651b-59de-aca5-26472ab8b179"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "0ab6fa70-a6cd-5cb7-9d73-3596ed01243a" :answer/id #uuid "0ab6fa70-a6cd-5cb7-9d73-3596ed01243a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 81 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "19c9f542-d2e2-5968-bded-427e0d8db6a3" :answer/id #uuid "19c9f542-d2e2-5968-bded-427e0d8db6a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "21f78820-0932-5d64-87e6-27386a744f06" :answer/id #uuid "21f78820-0932-5d64-87e6-27386a744f06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "249d1b9e-afcc-5de7-be41-64c8e545e5c1" :answer/id #uuid "249d1b9e-afcc-5de7-be41-64c8e545e5c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" :answer/id #uuid "728cf46f-850c-5bf2-8eee-8591a6dfdd10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id 