ned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["ebe1fb6b-3741-520d-8e93-709e401053a3"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" :answer/id #uuid "90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :answer/id #uuid "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "006d6dfe-0844-5971-9c61-382cbb28d0c2" :answer/id #uuid "006d6dfe-0844-5971-9c61-382cbb28d0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "5afa31f9-d938-51cb-b486-71ac3e40af68" :answer/id #uuid "5afa31f9-d938-51cb-b486-71ac3e40af68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 144 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3028c6da-4535-5dec-ad67-803e3189ac38" :answer/id #uuid "3028c6da-4535-5dec-ad67-803e3189ac38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "225 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb" :answer-field/id #uuid "1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["90fcb849-8f04-5003-8a20-d247a6ce6d3c" "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" "006d6dfe-0844-5971-9c61-382cbb28d0c2" "5afa31f9-d938-51cb-b486-71ac3e40af68" "3028c6da-4535-5dec-ad67-803e3189ac38"] :answer-field/correct "0a2e2e07-c0ef-5893-b301-92e3d7183221" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" :question/id #uuid "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=4$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(4\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(4\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=4}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 16\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 16)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[16 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(144 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=144 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["1194953f-36a6-55fd-86be-9f2bd4fd97cb"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "b517f91d-49bb-51a2-bb5d-82c3c6c010ad" :answer/id #uuid "b517f91d-49bb-51a2-bb5d-82c3c6c010ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :answer/id #uuid "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a5503768-564a-5162-9311-2202462dd105" :answer/id #uuid "a5503768-564a-5162-9311-2202462dd105" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c6e7d55b-c344-5ef3-a707-0a8e86cff164" :answer/id #uuid "c6e7d55b-c344-5ef3-a707-0a8e86cff164" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4fbae015-9c86-576e-9542-d658ec05d8b1" :answer/id #uuid "4fbae015-9c86-576e-9542-d658ec05d8b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 18 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c19f56d4-a3af-5c69-a1b9-2d85f2264a17" :answer-field/id #uuid "c19f56d4-a3af-5c69-a1b9-2d85f2264a17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["b517f91d-49bb-51a2-bb5d-82c3c6c010ad" "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" "a5503768-564a-5162-9311-2202462dd105" "c6e7d55b-c344-5ef3-a707-0a8e86cff164" "4fbae015-9c86-576e-9542-d658ec05d8b1"] :answer-field/correct "9419e38d-b9bd-570b-8181-283253610c4d" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" :question/id #uuid "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["c19f56d4-a3af-5c69-a1b9-2d85f2264a17"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "aaa3c0f4-aada-5874-bf25-4f4d4627107b" :answer/id #uuid "aaa3c0f4-aada-5874-bf25-4f4d4627107b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "34c4690f-7fa6-5adf-99a3-e7e66e0f4675" :answer/id #uuid "34c4690f-7fa6-5adf-99a3-e7e66e0f4675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :answer/id #uuid "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0e0d836e-54a3-5340-9b13-dcc36232e947" :answer/id #uuid "0e0d836e-54a3-5340-9b13-dcc36232e947" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "2058e903-53c1-5c81-9185-9c31af4d09f3" :answer/id #uuid "2058e903-53c1-5c81-9185-9c31af4d09f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x}\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 8 y^{2} + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["aaa3c0f4-aada-5874-bf25-4f4d4627107b" "34c4690f-7fa6-5adf-99a3-e7e66e0f4675" "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" "0e0d836e-54a3-5340-9b13-dcc36232e947" "2058e903-53c1-5c81-9185-9c31af4d09f3"] :answer-field/correct "9050d480-402e-54df-b100-9621e72db5ba" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" :question/id #uuid "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2 - x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["1111b70b-db9b-5b6e-800f-d01f0ede213c"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :answer/id #uuid "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "da7db780-8908-5832-a157-32ca134dc1bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "da7db780-8908-5832-a157-32ca134dc1bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2"] :answer-field/correct "db3e91b6-95a7-5cbd-970b-c036b38842f2" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" :question/id #uuid "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $\\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["da7db780-8908-5832-a157-32ca134dc1bc"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "3e5c3ddf-a226-5fb1-8ac1-23c3bb6fc29d" :answer/id #uuid "3e5c3ddf-a226-5fb1-8ac1-23c3bb6fc29d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "53fef7c3-564b-508d-bbe5-96ce9d4af498" :answer/id #uuid "53fef7c3-564b-508d-bbe5-96ce9d4af498" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x - 2}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :answer/id #uuid "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "5e5aff36-c237-5c29-96f2-1540d685c947" :answer/id #uuid "5e5aff36-c237-5c29-96f2-1540d685c947" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(3 x^{2} + 6 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "0ee86905-f590-503c-8720-85bf192e1221" :answer/id #uuid "0ee86905-f590-503c-8720-85bf192e1221" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(- 6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "279f0fe1-f8e4-5afa-b477-f2e27ef72c4d" :answer-field/id #uuid "279f0fe1-f8e4-5afa-b477-f2e27ef72c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["3e5c3ddf-a226-5fb1-8ac1-23c3bb6fc29d" "53fef7c3-564b-508d-bbe5-96ce9d4af498" "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" "5e5aff36-c237-5c29-96f2-1540d685c947" "0ee86905-f590-503c-8720-85bf192e1221"] :answer-field/correct "0bfd39c3-faa8-56f5-9d31-d7976ff31147" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" :question/id #uuid "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["279f0fe1-f8e4-5afa-b477-f2e27ef72c4d"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "5175042d-2d39-5599-87d4-e96b16f146f7" :answer/id #uuid "5175042d-2d39-5599-87d4-e96b16f146f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 450 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f64a0dba-2001-5ad1-8d0a-6f0446345425" :answer/id #uuid "f64a0dba-2001-5ad1-8d0a-6f0446345425" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 336 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "fcabb8f4-cd17-54eb-81e2-ebbe693a7554" :answer/id #uuid "fcabb8f4-cd17-54eb-81e2-ebbe693a7554" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "612 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :answer/id #uuid "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 612 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "36fade4b-e6ad-561d-a3f5-aa58e1863d13" :answer/id #uuid "36fade4b-e6ad-561d-a3f5-aa58e1863d13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "- 162 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3f987d97-8b0c-5075-a0a3-9441e76eccc0" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f987d97-8b0c-5075-a0a3-9441e76eccc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["5175042d-2d39-5599-87d4-e96b16f146f7" "f64a0dba-2001-5ad1-8d0a-6f0446345425" "fcabb8f4-cd17-54eb-81e2-ebbe693a7554" "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" "36fade4b-e6ad-561d-a3f5-aa58e1863d13"] :answer-field/correct "8215e715-ffec-5467-9a5e-17436a3528eb" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" :question/id #uuid "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["3f987d97-8b0c-5075-a0a3-9441e76eccc0"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :answer/id #uuid "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" :answer/id #uuid "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54 \\pi" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "09b21800-0e88-5dbb-a9a1-66f5cccf128e" :answer/id #uuid "09b21800-0e88-5dbb-a9a1-66f5cccf128e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-144" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "6b94038e-032c-5029-a77e-c204b7c5d549" :answer/id #uuid "6b94038e-032c-5029-a77e-c204b7c5d549" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "c8f9c2ea-45a4-530b-a88e-c343c3142e74" :answer/id #uuid "c8f9c2ea-45a4-530b-a88e-c343c3142e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "fce7b7e5-6f3c-584c-bca6-6514b9a1b744" :answer-field/id #uuid "fce7b7e5-6f3c-584c-bca6-6514b9a1b744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" "804436a9-64c7-584d-bd22-73da3b39a9ba" "09b21800-0e88-5dbb-a9a1-66f5cccf128e" "6b94038e-032c-5029-a77e-c204b7c5d549" "c8f9c2ea-45a4-530b-a88e-c343c3142e74"] :answer-field/correct "def1e612-15ff-5830-8d9a-f4fc5367e6d0" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76" :question/id #uuid "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2 x.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x\\right)=2\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle|_{z=2 x}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(0\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["fce7b7e5-6f3c-584c-bca6-6514b9a1b744"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "4b16d384-1b97-54b9-ace8-78618fbd0f73" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133927.py"} [:db/add 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/questions ["e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" "14caf42e-c7a2-5980-adda-a477fd9e1d63" "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76"]} [